this morning on the dish we head into  the kitchen with tyler aiken native of  wilmington delaware who's come home  after running  and opening nationally recognized  restaurants in washington in  philadelphia  he's putting a 21st century twist on a  historic landmark  and he's also keeping a table open for  another well-known wilmington native  president-elect joe biden  tyler aiken first stepped foot in the  green room at the historic hotel dupont  as a kid growing up in wilmington  delaware  this was the restaurant when you're  eight years old that you kind of didn't  want to go to  because you had to put on a blazer and  it was like classical music  but even then he could tell it was  something special  the space especially as a kid is so vast  you know and the the details so ornate  when i thought about  would i ever do a restaurant in delaware  i always had this  dream in my head that it would be this  restaurant  now he's the owner and head chef of la  cavier at the green room  transformed into a french brasserie what  akin didn't plan for  was opening a new restaurant in the  middle of a global pandemic  while running three more in nearby  philadelphia  two of them remain open but one recently  closed  we're all in the same boat as  restauranteurs it's just trying to  pivot adapt and survive at this point  he's channeling his concerns about the  pandemic into the independent  restaurants coalition  a group launched this year to represent  the roughly 500 000  independent restaurants nationwide  employing more than 11 million people  the group is led by well-known chefs  including jose andres  tom colicchio and thomas keller some of  these guys that you've partnered with  are people you've worked with before  competitors  probably people you want to emulate you  know some of my heroes  are on a zoom screen in front of me  every single day  all of the kind of star struck  aspect of it just melts away really  quickly because we're all there to  to get business done and what does  washington need to do  to help your industry quite simply they  need to pass the restaurants act  the legislation is passed in the house  but not the senate  it would provide about 120 billion  dollars in federal grants to restaurants  bars and other food companies  that had business dry up nearly  overnight once the pandemic  struck so many restaurants are closing  every day  and will have closed by the time this  thing has finally passed what happens to  a  community when the restaurant closes we  are entertainment  at the end of the day as much as we are  sustenance and  we stand to lose that  very important part of our local  cultures more importantly  we stand to lose jobs inspired by his  father aiken has been into politics  since he was a kid  and initially went to law school before  realizing he wanted to be a chef  he enrolled in the prestigious academia  cuisine  in washington so what do we have we've  got gnocchi parisian  french style gnocchi no potato involved  jumbo lump crab from the chesapeake  and then we gild the lily a little bit  with some uh with some caviar  that sounds amazing and then there's  cake french apple cider cake we make it  with like a farmhouse  northern french alcohol exciter a  uh galvados creme glaze and then if  calvados caramel pecans on there  aiken has worked in renowned kitchens  inspired by mediterranean and thai  flavors like comey and little cerro in  dc  and by israel like zahav in philadelphia  how do you get from all that to a french  brasserie  my dad's side is very french i wanted to  find  new ways to approach this cuisine that  drew from  my own personal history why do you try  to draw  influences from all those stops i've  made along the way take the collier's  grilled swordfish which is served with  whipped fenna  and a tzatziki made with butternut  squash  the whipped feta is something that you  know is a comey thing for sure  doing something like putting squash in  the tzatziki is  something that like reminds me of a lot  of the stuff we did at tahoe it was  often taking a traditional thing  like ted ziki and like doing it a little  differently he's also updated the  historic space  all these memories in this restaurant we  needed to honor people who've  gotten engaged here celebrated  anniversaries birthdays  it was like a balancing act between  updating it  making it commercially viable but also  preserving the integrity of the  restaurant keeping it recognizable  built in 1913 the hotel dupont has  hosted presidents  movie stars and world leaders co-owner  rob buccini and his firm  bought the property in 2013 from the  dupont chemical company  it's been the anchor of a nearly 2  billion dollar redevelopment of downtown  and of joe biden's political life  but you don't think of yourself as  anything other than the story when you  come in here  it's been you know part of delaware  history and  obviously part of the the biden history  as well for a lot of people  it is the hotel dupont but it's also the  biden hotel it's that place where he  always seems to do  big things yes in 1972 then 29 year old  joe biden celebrated his first  senate election night victory there more  recently the ballrooms were settings for  speeches  and campaign produced videos buccini is  hoping that the hotel's presidential  connection  helps once the pandemic ends we think  that the biden effect will be  significant  that people will come to visit  wilmington  find out how great a place it is the  newly reopened restaurant has yet to get  a visit from wilmington's most famous  resident  thanks to the pandemic has he been here  yet we expect to see him  but you hold the table for him  absolutely  yum we wish you brought the cake i know  so you take the  seller down to wilmington i'll take this  from dc  we'll meet there by the way real quick  have you ever had street closures for  your dish  no you had we had the street closed so  that we could do that outside so special  thanks to the wilmington police  department
 

